WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.969
A shared cluster runs many machine-learning

2
00:00:03.031 --> 00:00:03.900
training jobs.

3
00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:06.465
Every round, here two minutes

4
00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:08.385
a scheduler chooses which

5
00:00:08.459 --> 00:00:10.010
jobs run on the GPUs.

6
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.109
Many jobs change their batch

7
00:00:12.172 --> 00:00:14.920
size as they train, and with it their speed.

8
00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.388
Shockwave, from NSDI 2023

9
00:00:17.388 --> 00:00:19.914
plans ahead for those changes

10
00:00:19.995 --> 00:00:22.765
to finish jobs fast while treating

11
00:00:22.847 --> 00:00:24.150
each one fairly.

12
00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:27.770
The task judges a schedule on four counts.

13
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.000
Makespan: when the last job finishes.

14
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.350
Average job completion time.

15
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.299
The worst finish-time fairness, called rho

16
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.233
a job's completion time divided

17
00:00:38.294 --> 00:00:40.228
by the time it would take with a

18
00:00:40.288 --> 00:00:41.860
fair share of the cluster.

19
00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.199
And the share of unfair jobs

20
00:00:44.199 --> 00:00:46.530
with rho above one point oh five.

21
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:47.423
To pass

22
00:00:47.491 --> 00:00:50.072
a run must beat the authors' scheduler

23
00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.567
strictly on all four

24
00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:54.080
on both hidden traces of eight jobs.

25
00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.318
Here is a small cluster of our own

26
00:00:57.318 --> 00:00:59.140
four GPUs and six jobs.

27
00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.184
Each job needs a fixed number of GPUs

28
00:01:02.184 --> 00:01:05.090
and its epochs get shorter as it trains.

29
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.377
A policy ranks the live jobs

30
00:01:07.444 --> 00:01:10.049
and each round the simulator packs them

31
00:01:10.116 --> 00:01:12.320
onto the free GPUs in that order.

32
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.980
Try three simple policies.

33
00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:16.030
First come

34
00:01:16.094 --> 00:01:18.323
first served finishes everything at

35
00:01:18.386 --> 00:01:20.551
two thousand four hundred seconds

36
00:01:20.615 --> 00:01:22.780
but its worst job is two point six

37
00:01:22.844 --> 00:01:24.690
seven times slower than fair.

38
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.125
Shortest remaining time first has

39
00:01:27.188 --> 00:01:29.463
the shortest average completion time

40
00:01:29.526 --> 00:01:31.232
and the fewest unfair jobs

41
00:01:31.232 --> 00:01:32.622
but it finishes last

42
00:01:32.622 --> 00:01:34.960
at two thousand five hundred twenty.

43
00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:36.601
The fairest policy

44
00:01:36.669 --> 00:01:38.775
which always runs the job whose

45
00:01:38.843 --> 00:01:40.677
fairness would suffer most

46
00:01:40.677 --> 00:01:42.308
has the best worst rho

47
00:01:42.308 --> 00:01:43.870
one point three three.

48
00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.525
Yet every one of its six jobs ends

49
00:01:46.593 --> 00:01:48.220
above one point oh five.

50
00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.429
Each policy wins somewhere

51
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:51.930
and loses somewhere.

52
00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:54.110
Pause and predict.

53
00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.852
Shortest remaining time first is known

54
00:01:56.914 --> 00:01:59.180
to minimize average completion time.

55
00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:01.530
Will it also win the other three?

56
00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.843
You just saw the answer

57
00:02:03.843 --> 00:02:05.680
it won two and lost two.

58
00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:08.493
Beating a reference on all four at once

59
00:02:08.556 --> 00:02:10.830
is much harder than winning any one.

60
00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:15.525
On the real task

61
00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:17.426
Claude Opus 5.5 kept its own

62
00:02:17.491 --> 00:02:19.196
copy of every job's state

63
00:02:19.262 --> 00:02:22.081
copied the simulator's rules for batch-size

64
00:02:22.146 --> 00:02:24.048
changes to forecast each job

65
00:02:24.113 --> 00:02:26.211
and searched over job orders for

66
00:02:26.277 --> 00:02:27.850
the best combined score.

67
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.721
It had the best fairness

68
00:02:29.785 --> 00:02:31.052
of all sixteen runs

69
00:02:31.052 --> 00:02:33.524
twelve point five percent unfair jobs

70
00:02:33.588 --> 00:02:35.046
against sixty-two point

71
00:02:35.109 --> 00:02:36.440
five for the authors.

72
00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.116
But its makespan was sixteen thousand

73
00:02:39.179 --> 00:02:40.561
seven hundred seconds

74
00:02:40.561 --> 00:02:42.950
against sixteen thousand one hundred

75
00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:43.830
so it failed.

76
00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.676
Its score, forty-seven point one

77
00:02:46.676 --> 00:02:48.340
was third of sixteen.

78
00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:50.210
Four models passed.

79
00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.614
That ends the Network lecture

80
00:02:52.614 --> 00:02:53.830
with three lessons.

81
00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:55.867
Find the wait: in BBQ

82
00:02:55.943 --> 00:02:58.090
both passing designs removed

83
00:02:58.167 --> 00:03:01.540
the same memory wait, in two different ways.

84
00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.886
Generalize past the feedback

85
00:03:03.955 --> 00:03:05.951
the hidden workloads included

86
00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.810
cases no agent could test.

87
00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.907
And every metric counts: in Shockwave

88
00:03:10.907 --> 00:03:14.160
the fairest run of all failed on one number.

89
00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:15.477
As an exercise

90
00:03:15.542 --> 00:03:18.186
try to write a policy that beats shortest

91
00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:20.830
remaining time first on all four counts.

92
00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.413
The interactive notes for this lecture

93
00:03:23.471 --> 00:03:24.940
have every model we used.

94
00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:28.490
Next time: Lecture 5, theory.
